Start();
include(unitTests);
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking UnitTests Utilities list;
register(BrkT);
define
    bkN=0;
    bkBump argument code={
        bkN = bkN + 1;
        };
    bkWalk argument code={
        iterate bkCur members on *argument;
        while ++bkCur;
            if bkN == 3;    break;
            bkBump(bkCur);
        };
    bkRun code={
        bkWalk(Grokking);
        print "BREAK SPECIMEN bkN =" bkN:;
        print "BREAK SPECIMEN SENTINEL":;
        stop();
        };
    ;
bkRun();

THE RUNG-ONE SPECIMEN: a minimal program whose parse contains a `break`.

It exists to be parsed, and its OUTPUT is the oracle -- the interpreter's
product against which a bound generated parsebreak must be compared. Captured
BEFORE any bind, per the rung's own rule, so the comparison cannot be taken
against a baseline the treatment already touched.

The walk stops at 3 rather than running to completion, so the `break` is not
decoration: a run in which the break did not fire prints a different number.
That is the anti-vacuity guard -- a specimen whose break could be deleted
without changing the output would prove nothing about break's parse.

⚠ The cursor `bkCur` is deliberately NOT declared (bear-trap 38).
